age":{"dependencies":[],"digest":"1ed77ccf25416a20d2a0744ee45964ee32efaa74f8788e8a8f156f40ed5c5902"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:T10","usage":{"input_tokens":70157,"output_tokens":2706,"requests":3}},{"kind":"success","lineage":{"dependencies":[],"digest":"0443ea4f92f51169a44f9bf42a41a12aa5aee9a25135f7c71cabf713a8671f4b"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:G6","usage":{"input_tokens":191901,"output_tokens":5481,"requests":5}}],"version":1},"findings":[{"block_threshold":0.8,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"State that the new `proxy_redirect https://$host/ https://$host/review/;` rule only restores the prefix when the upstream emits https:// Locations (a61d's FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS layer), note that a61d's earlier `http://$host/` rule shadows the http:// case by first-match-wins, and record the atomic-co-deploy of both changes as a load-bearing precondition (ideally with a test asserting the http:// path is out of scope / covered by the https-emission guarantee).","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.69,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'the new rule's https://$host/ prefix overlaps none of the existing rules (they match http://…), so it neither shadows nor is shadowed by them.'","infra/nginx/rebar.conf.template:118 `proxy_redirect http://$host/ https://$host/;` is declared BEFORE the appended rule; nginx uses the first matching proxy_redirect, so an http://$host/health Location is consumed by :118 and rewritten to https://$host/health (no /review/) before the new rule is reached.","Ticket a61d AC5 + bug 1d1b: a61d's FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS root-cause layer is merged but NOT live, and the nginx template cannot auto-deploy; the currently-deployed app still emits http:// Locations, which is exactly the shadowed case."],"finding":"The single new rule `proxy_redirect https://$host/ https://$host/review/;` restores the /review/ prefix ONLY when the upstream emits an https:// absolute Location; for an http://$host/... Location, a61d's earlier rule `http://$host/ -> https://$host/` matches first (first-match-wins) and rewrites to https://$host/health, dropping the prefix, and the new rule never fires. The plan's non-overlap argument ('overlaps none of the existing rules') holds for https:// but silently omits that the http:// case is shadowed, so the fix's correctness depends on a61d's FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS (https-emission) being live simultaneously — a precondition not stated as load-bearing.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"ffdc2668bc56bb6dc","impact":0.33,"is_primary":null,"location":"Chosen approach (Option 1) — the new proxy_redirect rule; and Root cause section ('Post-a61d the app emits https://$host/<route>')","norm_id":"ne62f63c27963a819","priority":0.11,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Any deploy state where the app still emits http://$host/<route> (FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS not yet effective) but the new nginx rule is live: prefix silently lost with correct scheme, /health falls through to Gerrit and 404s — the exact symptom this ticket fixes."],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"In the Chosen-approach section, add a sentence making the precondition explicit: 'This rule fires only for https:// Locations; a61d's earlier http://$host/ -> https://$host/ rule (declared first) handles/consumes the http:// case, so prefix restoration is contingent on the upstream emitting https (a61d's FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS), and both must go live in the same edge-deploy.' Optionally consider a rule that also covers the http:// case ahead of a61d's http->https rule (e.g. `proxy_redirect http://$host/ https://$host/review/;` ordered before the plain scheme upgrade) so the prefix survives regardless of emission scheme.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.3333,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_un